  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chilly through Sunday morning with a few lingering \nrain and wet snow showers across higher terrain this morning.\n\nChilly NNW flow will continue this morning, with lake induced \ninstability and upslope flow supporting a few widely scattered \nshowers with the best coverage across higher terrain. Cooling \nsurface and boundary layer temps may allow a few wet snowflakes to \nmix in across higher terrain briefly around daybreak before the \nshowers end, but there will be no accumulation. Lake effect and \nupslope clouds will linger this morning, but strong subsidence and \ndrying will then allow for rapid clearing with plenty of sunshine by \nthis afternoon. The cold airmass will only support highs in the mid \nto upper 40s for lower elevations and lower 40s higher terrain \ndespite the afternoon sun.\n\nHigh pressure will build from the Ohio Valley into PA and the Mid \nAtlantic tonight, with associated dry air and subsidence bringing \nclear skies to our region. The clear skies will certainly support \nradiational cooling overnight, but with the high centered south of \nthe region a light breeze may persist in most areas and limit \nradiational cooling to some extent. A Freeze Warning has been issued \nfor areas where the growing season is still active, but the prospect \nof a light breeze persisting lowers confidence that temps will drop \nbelow freezing in some areas.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An extended period of dry and warmer weather begins \nSunday afternoon and likely lasts through next weekend.\n\nStrong high pressure will drift to the Mid Atlantic and New England \nby Sunday afternoon, with southwest return flow quickly removing the \nchilly airmass from the region. Highs will reach the lower 60s for \nlower elevations and mid 50s for higher terrain and the North \nCountry after a chilly start.\n\nSunday will begin an extended stretch of dry and sunny weather that \nwill likely last all the way through next weekend. High pressure \nsurface and aloft will take up residence across much of the central \nand eastern CONUS, with the pattern stagnating for an extended \nperiod of time. This will yield dry weather and plenty of sunshine \neach day for the foreseeable future. A cutoff low will be trapped \nwithin the ridge just off the Carolina and Mid Atlantic coast all \nweek, but model and ensemble consensus keeps this system well \nremoved from the eastern Great Lakes.",
